---
import Layout from '../layouts/ResourceLayout.astro';
import ArrivalPatterns from '../components/learning/ArrivalPatterns.astro';
import QueueExperiment from '../components/learning/QueueExperiment.astro';
import TwoCycleExperiment from '../components/learning/TwoCycleExperiment.astro';
import QueueInitiative from '../components/learning/QueueInitiative.astro';
import IntersectionPhaseView from '../components/learning/IntersectionPhaseView.astro';
import intersection from '../../../project/v3/illustrations/four-way-intersection.jpg';
import '../styles/v3-learning.css';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Layout title="Intersection model" section="Intersection model" heading="Explore a traffic-light intersection"
  description="Use a small executable model to explore how traffic arriving at a signalised intersection can form queues—and how a team can learn from the result.">
  <div class="intersection-workspace chapter-body">
    <nav class="workspace-nav" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#the-case">The case</a><a href="#phase-title">Signal cycle</a><a href="#arrival-experiment">Move an arrival</a><a href="#two-cycle-title">Follow two cycles</a><a href="#shared-information">Return to the initiative</a>
    </nav>
    <section id="the-case">
      <div class="case-overview">
        <div>
          <h2>The engineering question</h2>
          <p>A city wants to improve how people move through a four-way intersection. The team needs to understand how signal phases and arrival patterns affect queues while considering the people who use the junction.</p>
          <p>This simplified model follows vehicles in two directions: north–south (NS) and east–west (EW). The scenarios use invented arrivals and a fixed signal sequence, not measured traffic.</p>
        </div>
        <img src={intersection.src} alt="Illustration of a four-way traffic-light intersection" loading="eager" />
      </div>
      <h3>How the model works</h3>
      <p>Each cycle has eight abstract slots, not seconds. In each slot, arrivals join their queue first; at most one vehicle leaves the direction with a green signal. During amber and all-red slots, neither direction is served. The model records the queues after each slot.</p>
      <p>It does not model pedestrians, turning movements, real signal timings, failures or traffic safety. A small queue result alone cannot establish that a design is safe.</p>
      <details><summary>Inspect the fixed signal sequence and invented arrivals</summary><ArrivalPatterns /></details>
    </section>
    <IntersectionPhaseView />
    <QueueExperiment />
    <section aria-labelledby="two-cycle-heading">
      <h2 id="two-cycle-heading">Carry the queues forward</h2>
      <p>What happens at the boundary between cycles? Continue the same fixed schedule and arrival pattern, carrying both remaining queues into the next cycle.</p>
      <TwoCycleExperiment />
    </section>
    <section id="shared-information">
      <h2>Return the learning to the initiative</h2>
      <p>The experiment can inform a team’s next question. The excerpts below show the shared initiative before the worked comparison and a proposed update afterwards. The proposal is for team review, not an automatically accepted decision.</p>
      <details><summary>Compare the initiative before and after</summary><QueueInitiative stage="before" /><QueueInitiative stage="after" /></details>
      <p>An AI agent created the runnable model from the course's documented intent, traffic assumptions and rules. An agent could adapt or rebuild it as the inputs or questions change; the results can inform updates to the shared information and the initiative. This page runs the maintained implementation and does not automatically regenerate model code. <a href={base + 'learn/v3/05-learning-through-models-and-prototypes/'}>Chapter 5 explains the learning loop</a>.</p>
      <p><a href={base + 'learn/v3/02-introducing-the-engineering-challenge/'}>Meet the intersection in Chapter 2</a> · <a href={base + 'learn/v3/06-engineering-data-from-measurements-to-decisions/'}>Follow the evidence in Chapter 6</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
  .intersection-workspace { --bg: var(--home-bg); --panel: var(--home-panel); --text: var(--home-text); --muted: var(--home-muted); --rule: var(--home-rule); --teal: var(--home-teal); --gold: var(--home-gold); --mono: var(--home-mono); }
  .workspace-nav { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .6rem 1.2rem; padding-block: 1rem; border-block: 1px solid var(--home-rule); }
  .case-overview { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.1fr .9fr; gap: 2rem; align-items: center; }
  .case-overview img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: .4rem; }
  .intersection-workspace :global(table) { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
  .intersection-workspace :global(th), .intersection-workspace :global(td) { padding: .5rem; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--home-rule); }
  .intersection-workspace :global(figure) { margin-inline: 0; }
  .intersection-workspace :global(#arrival-patterns) { overflow-x: auto; }
  .intersection-workspace :global(fieldset) { min-width: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) { .case-overview { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
